+# Daily Grind rewards registration
+
+The homepage includes a rewards registration form backed by `POST /api/verify-email`.
+It uses the experimental browser Email Verification API: the browser fills a hidden
+`autocomplete="email-verification-token"` input when a supported email provider can
+prove ownership. No OTP, magic link, or verification email is sent.
+
+## Deployment
+
+- Use Node.js 24 or later and install dependencies with `pnpm install --frozen-lockfile`.
+- Configure the environment variables in `.env.example` in your hosting environment.
+  The server does not automatically load `.env` files.
+- Set `PUBLIC_ORIGIN` to the exact externally visible HTTPS origin. Neither forwarded
+  headers nor the request Host header are trusted as the token audience.
+- Register that origin for the [Chrome Email Verification origin trial](https://developer.chrome.com/docs/identity/email-verification)
+  and set `EMAIL_VERIFICATION_ORIGIN_TRIAL` to the issued token. Keep it renewed.
+  The server emits it in the `Origin-Trial` response header.
+- Deploy with the `pnpm start` command behind an HTTPS reverse proxy. Local HTTP is
+  permitted only for localhost development. The default port is 11222.
+- Give the application a persistent, private `data/` directory. SQLite holds account
+  records and expiring signup sessions. This implementation targets one application
+  instance; multi-instance hosting needs a shared transactional database and limiter.
+- `pnpm build` performs syntax checks without starting the server or importing its entrypoint.
+
+Serving `index.html` alone is insufficient: each page render needs a server-generated
+nonce and session cookie. The Node server serves the page, script, and API; it never
+exposes the database or source files. Existing menu/account/order URLs remain outside
+this registration feature. Rewards earning, account login, and redemption are not added.
+
+## Verification behavior
+
+Each form receives a random 256-bit nonce stored in a server-side session, with a
+10-minute expiry. Its opaque cookie is HttpOnly, SameSite=Lax and Secure over HTTPS.
+Pages use `Cache-Control: no-store`. Submission atomically consumes the nonce, even
+on failure, and each response renders a fresh one. Origin checks and request limits
+protect the endpoint. Form submissions remain native so the browser can insert its
+verification token at submission time; registration works without JavaScript.
+
+The verifier uses `@sd-jwt/core` and `jose` to check the issuer signature, verified
+email claim (case-insensitively), expiration/freshness, asymmetric algorithm allowlist,
+browser key signature, audience, nonce, and SD-JWT hash binding. Before requesting
+issuer metadata or keys it checks the email domain's DNS TXT delegation. Outbound
+HTTPS requests reject private addresses and redirects, pin their DNS lookup, and
+have response-size and time limits. Issuer keys without `kid` are tried individually.
+
+Only successfully verified registrations are persisted. Passwords are salted and
+scrypt-hashed; raw tokens and passwords are never logged or retained. Duplicate
+accounts cannot overwrite existing credentials. Marketing is opt-in.
+
+Unsupported browsers, unsigned-in providers, missing tokens, and invalid tokens
+produce an inline retry message and never mark an email verified. The usual
+OTP/link fallback is deliberately omitted because this feature must not send either.
+Consequently, registration requires a participating browser/provider and an active
+origin-trial configuration. No universal browser-only ownership check exists.
+
+Protocol reference: [Email Verification Protocol draft 02](https://www.ietf.org/archive/id/draft-hardt-email-verification-02.html).
+
+## Validation performed
+
+Syntax checks only. No dev/preview server, browser walkthrough, server-entrypoint
+import, or live issuer exchange was run. End-to-end verification requires the deployed
+HTTPS origin, its origin-trial token, and a signed-in participating email account.
